// spec: CLS-014
//
// ⚠️ A defect is a statement about the INTERPRETER and is never compiled.
//
// CLS-014 requires 'X' is not a class. -- the shape the other inheritance
// errors use.  The message is 'Only instances have properties.' instead, which
// names neither the class, nor the superclass, nor inheritance, and describes
// a property access the program never wrote.
//
// ⚠️ The WriteLn above the declaration is deliberate: it prints, and the one
// below does not, which shows the check fires AT THE DECLARATION and not at
// construction.  The first draft of this case asserted the opposite, and
// running it is what corrected the rule.

var X := 1;

WriteLn ('reached the declaration');

class C (X);
begin
end

WriteLn ('never reached, and the class is never constructed');
